1937 again as reference 
To prepare for the 1937 season, Cohen played on various El Paso sandlot baseball clubs to learn how to throw a screwball.    During Senators spring training he showed off his screwball, [along with curve submarine.    ] stating that “I have confidence in that pitch.” [when referencing the screwball.]  He also began throwing a submarine curve.
Used exclusively in relief – his one start was rained out after four innings with Washington ahead, 4-0 – Cohen went 2-4, with a commendable 3.11 ERA in 55 innings pitched for the 1937 Senators. Season highlights included 1937 is his first full season with the Senators, primary as a relief pitcher. His only start was a rainout game against Cleveland, that ended in the fourth inning with Senators leading 4-0.      He also faces Bob Feller, Syd earns a win and also singles off the future HOF pitcher. [Barry: Check this: According to Retrosheet, Cohen saved an 11-2 win for starter Carl Fischer on June 23; he also hit a seventh-inning single off young reliever Feller.]  At the end of the season, the sale of Cohen’s contract to the International League Baltimore Orioles brought his brief major league career to a close. Over parts of three Washington campaigns, he posted a 3-7 (.300) record, with a 4.54 ERA in 109 innings thrown. Over that span, he surrendered 133 base hits and 37 walks while striking out 49. With the stick, Cohen batted a meek (5-for-32) .152 but fielded his position capably with a .980 FA in his 55 mound appearances. [he is sent to Chattanooga, but the Senators then sold to him to the Baltimore Orioles, of the International League.  Creating a squabble between the two teams on who should get the money from the sale.    ]

